'''Albert Einstein''' ([[March 14]], [[1879]] - [[April 18]], [[1955]]) was a [[physicist]] and [[mathematician]] who first proposed the [[theory of relativity]]. He was awarded the [[1921]] [[Nobel Prize in Physics|Nobel Prize for Physics]] for his explanation of the [[photoelectric effect]] "and other contributions"; however, the announcement of the award was not made until a year later, in [[1922]]. His theoretical work suggested the possibility of creating an [[nuclear weapon|atomic bomb]]. His discovered equation
:<math> E = m c^{2} </math>
is well known as one that changed the world.
